<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arthur Schopenhauer</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1788">1788</a>–<a href="/years/1860">1860</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Schopenhauer</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Arthur Schopenhauer was a German philosopher and writer, born in Danzig, known for The World as Will and Representation (1818, expanded in 1844), which describes the world of appearances as the manifestation of a blind and irrational will. Building on the transcendental idealism of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/immanuel-kant">Immanuel Kant</a>, he developed an atheistic, metaphysical and ethical system that rejected the German idealism of his day, and he was among the first Western philosophers to affirm tenets of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-philosophy">Indian philosophy</a> such as asceticism and denial of the self. His work drew little attention during his lifetime. He settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> in 1833 and lived there until his death in 1860.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/arthur-schopenhauer.jpg" alt="Arthur Schopenhauer" width="330" height="422" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Johann Schäfer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arthur_Schopenhauer_by_J_Schäfer,_1859b.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Schopenhauer was born on 22 February 1788 in Danzig, then part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth</a>, to Heinrich Floris Schopenhauer and Johanna Schopenhauer, both from wealthy patrician families. They supported the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a> and were republicans and Anglophiles. When Danzig became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in 1793 Heinrich moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a>, a free city with a republican constitution. In 1797 Arthur was sent to Le Havre, where he learned French, and from 1799 he played the flute.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar">Weimar</a> his relationship with his mother deteriorated. Her salon was frequented by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-wolfgang-von-goethe">Johann Wolfgang von Goethe</a>, who seemed not to notice the young student. In 1813 he left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> in a rush, fearing he would be pressed into military service, and settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudolstadt">Rudolstadt</a>, where he wrote his dissertation On the Fourfold Root of the Principle of Sufficient Reason.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>In November 1813 Goethe invited him to help with the Theory of Colours. Schopenhauer thought colour theory minor, but the work led him to a demonstration of the a priori nature of causality. He saw his philosophy as an extension of Kant's. The first sentence of his main work reads &quot;The world is my representation&quot;: everything that exists for cognition exists as an object for a subject.</p><p>In its second book he argues that the world as it is in itself is will, a blind striving that he held condemns the individual to a life of suffering without final purpose. Aesthetic contemplation offers a temporary escape, in which one becomes a &quot;pure, will-less, painless, timeless, subject of cognition&quot;. In ethics, a compassionate person makes less distinction between himself and others. He named Kant, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upanishads">Upanishads</a> as his chief influences.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In August 1821 Schopenhauer had an incident with his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> neighbour, the seamstress Caroline Louise Marquet. He said he had pushed her from his entrance and that she fell purposely so that she could sue; she said he had attacked her so violently that she was paralysed on her right side. The court found him guilty in May 1827 and ordered him to pay her a pension until her death in 1842.</p><p>Schopenhauer remained the most influential German philosopher until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. Friedrich Nietzsche and Karl Robert Eduard von Hartmann took his philosophy as a starting point. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-einstein">Albert Einstein</a> described his thoughts as a &quot;continual consolation&quot; and hung his portrait in his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> study with those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-faraday">Michael Faraday</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-clerk-maxwell">James Clerk Maxwell</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Arthur Schopenhauer” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Hindu philosophy, from the Upanishads to Vedanta, influenced thinkers in Europe and America from the 19th century onwards, among them <b>Arthur Schopenhauer</b> and the American Transcendentalists.</blockquote><a href="/religion/hinduism">Hinduism · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1904 the scholar Wang Guowei wrote an essay that invoked <b>Arthur Schopenhauer</b>, called the novel the tragedy of tragedies, in contrast to the prosperous endings in most earlier drama and fiction, and proclaimed it worthy of being considered the one great masterpiece in the realm of Chinese…</blockquote><a href="/work/dream-of-the-red-chamber">Dream of the Red Chamber · Reception</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Every man takes the limits of his own field of vision for the limits of the world. This is an error of the intellect as inevitable as that error of the eye which lets us fancy that on the horizon heaven and earth meet.&#8221;</p><footer>&quot;Psychological Observations&quot;, Studies in Pessimism &#183; tr. T. Bailey Saunders, 1891 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/10732/pg10732-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Health outweighs all other blessings so much that one may really say that a healthy beggar is happier than an ailing king.&#8221;</p><footer>The Wisdom of Life, chapter 2 &#183; tr. T. Bailey Saunders, 1890 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/10741/pg10741-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
